# Prospective Automation routing recovery corrections

This isolated stage starts from the frozen production routing receipt `b85b20796ca6c029ff37cc955e91b62b547ec493721dad4a6342413ddfa03801`. Its independent read-only review and exact original defect observations remain preserved under `review-before/`; production source and historical receipts are untouched.

The prospective native core rechecks the cooperative active deadline after the awaited pre-node checkpoint, before executing the node. A checkpoint or blocked I/O is allowed to finish; this does not preempt blocked I/O or impose an elapsed wall-clock deadline. Existing cancellation precedence is retained.

For the bounded acyclic graph containing Merge, recovery also validates that persisted routing outputs have supported port/item shapes and that each pending invocation or arrived Merge buffer equals its completed predecessor's output batch. A deferred Wait output has not arrived. Already-arrived nonempty batches cannot disappear into empty/null input slots. The enabled Manual invocation retains its initial empty-input form. This is local state consistency checking, not authentication against coordinated rewriting of a complete checkpoint or an integrity/durability guarantee.

Genuine serialized partial joins, full Merge invocations queued behind an independent Wait and fan-out pending beside a deferred Wait are covered explicitly. Original Switch and Merge primitive code remains byte-identical to production. The focused run includes the existing 56 native/routing/original groups, three existing editor groups and fifteen new finite recovery/HTTP regressions, including actual isolated shared-host rejection with zero downstream requests. Original comparison accounting remains separate from test-group counts.

The shared original development cache is referenced read-only by an ignored stage junction. The selected routing originals are copied into the stage's own ignored cache; synthetic oracle observations are stage-owned. Do not run acquisition through the shared cache junction. No production app/results, shared runner, metadata or user browser ports are modified.

Remaining limits include the previously documented finite node/version/expression subset, no full released scheduler/server/database/editor/SQL execution, no arbitrary queue generations or cycles, no whole-product parity, no elapsed I/O preemption and no broad security/reliability/performance claim. Historical and failed source versions stay exact; failed test setup is distinguished from runtime behavior.

The HTTP item loop and every redirect hop recheck context cancellation/active deadline and the per-item controller timeout before Fetch. The 500-request budget continues to count prepared input-item attempts, including a guard-rejected attempt; redirect hops are separately limited to 0–10 configured hops per item, and share that item's timeout. A completed prior request cannot be undone. Aggregate NATIVE_LIMIT rejection remains distinct from an ordinary request timer error, with caller cancellation taking precedence.

The recovery check models immediate queue-to-recorded-output consistency in the bounded acyclic one-edge-per-input graph. It does not prove the complete historical graph prefix, authenticate trace/item-link semantics, or prevent coordinated rewriting of outputs and queued data. JSON batch comparison follows the persisted JSON representation; it does not promise non-JSON identity/alias, undefined or signed-zero lexical fidelity. Cached source representations avoid reserializing a shared fan-out source; this remains a bounded development check, not process-memory isolation.

The initial 69/69 checkpoint is retained under historical/recovery-checkpoint-69.json.snapshot with exact source snapshots in its run directory. Five additional missing HTTP-boundary guards were reproduced against those authenticated bytes using owned Fetch/monotonic-clock fixtures. These fixtures deliberately ignore AbortSignal to isolate explicit calls; they do not claim that standard Fetch ignores abort or establish remote delivery. The initial failed fan-out test built the wrong queue order; its exact source/raw run remains preserved, and only the synthetic fixture edges were corrected. No acceptance or performance threshold was relaxed.
